1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:01,517
Hi everyone,

2
00:00:01,537 --> 00:00:07,140
and welcome back to Sewing Machine Design and Manufacturing at WenQuest.

3
00:00:07,540 --> 00:00:13,760
This is Lecture 26, connected machines and digital twins.

4
00:00:14,160 --> 00:00:20,548
{'In the background is a quarter-hour of events from one lockstitch machine':

5
00:00:20,568 --> 00:00:25,292
"when the spindle turned, every trim, every thread break.

6
00:00:25,312 --> 00:00:30,784
Today we'll see how those numbers get out, how much of it to send,

7
00:00:30,804 --> 00:00:33,198
and what to turn them into."}

8
00:00:34,718 --> 00:00:36,698
Here's the problem.

9
00:00:37,098 --> 00:00:44,558
A shirt line fits a connectivity module to the lockstitch machine at station 7.

10
00:00:44,958 --> 00:00:52,333
A 480-minute shift with a 30-minute break, 0.6 standard minutes a piece,

11
00:00:52,353 --> 00:00:58,084
85 percent efficiency; 25 minutes waiting, 12 of faults,

12
00:00:58,104 --> 00:01:02,603
1.5 thread breaks an hour, 3 percent rework.

13
00:01:03,003 --> 00:01:06,503
The shop plans to count pieces by trims.

14
00:01:06,903 --> 00:01:08,763
How many pieces really?

15
00:01:09,163 --> 00:01:11,303
What OEE?

16
00:01:11,703 --> 00:01:14,327
How far off is the trim count?

17
00:01:14,727 --> 00:01:19,987
And if 500 machines reported every stitch, how much data a year?

18
00:01:20,387 --> 00:01:21,807
We'll answer at the end.

19
00:01:23,327 --> 00:01:27,939
Where the data come from and how to organise them.

20
00:01:29,459 --> 00:01:31,679
Where do the data come from?

21
00:01:32,079 --> 00:01:39,105
To do its own job the controller already records spindle start, stop and speed,

22
00:01:39,125 --> 00:01:43,208
stitch count, trims, backtacks and foot lifts,

23
00:01:43,228 --> 00:01:49,897
thread breaks on machines with a detector, plus fault codes and parameters,

24
00:01:49,917 --> 00:01:51,859
with no extra sensors.

25
00:01:52,259 --> 00:01:58,151
But it doesn't know who is sewing or which piece: the operator swipes a badge,

26
00:01:58,171 --> 00:02:02,319
and the piece comes from a ticket barcode or hanger ID.

27
00:02:02,719 --> 00:02:08,866
Put both together and you know who did which operation on which piece,

28
00:02:08,886 --> 00:02:09,659
and when.

29
00:02:11,179 --> 00:02:15,634
A floor often has machines from several makers and generations,

30
00:02:15,654 --> 00:02:18,830
so their data must first share one structure.

31
00:02:19,230 --> 00:02:24,436
Four kinds: identity, such as ID, model, firmware and station,

32
00:02:24,456 --> 00:02:29,662
which rarely changes; state, mapped with OPC UA for Machinery,

33
00:02:29,682 --> 00:02:33,876
the other main industrial standard, running, idle,

34
00:02:33,896 --> 00:02:40,366
fault and off matching its four states; counters, quantities that only go up;

35
00:02:40,386 --> 00:02:44,159
and events, things that happen at an instant.

36
00:02:45,679 --> 00:02:51,139
The key with counters: report cumulative values, not increments.

37
00:02:51,539 --> 00:02:56,999
Here the machine reports the shift's running total every minute.

38
00:02:57,399 --> 00:03:00,661
The 10:02 message is lost,

39
00:03:00,681 --> 00:03:08,359
yet 124 at 10:03 minus 121 at 10:01 still gives all 3 pieces,

40
00:03:08,379 --> 00:03:12,019
and a duplicate does no harm.

41
00:03:12,419 --> 00:03:17,227
Report 'pieces this minute' and a lost message is lost for good.

42
00:03:17,627 --> 00:03:25,593
The receiver must also spot resets after a power cycle or controller swap and

43
00:03:25,613 --> 00:03:26,527
carry on.

44
00:03:28,047 --> 00:03:32,502
Communication: MQTT and OPC UA.

45
00:03:34,022 --> 00:03:38,900
One of the most used protocols on the floor is MQTT,

46
00:03:38,920 --> 00:03:41,819
which works like a post office.

47
00:03:42,219 --> 00:03:48,743
A machine publishes to a topic, say 'line 3 station 7 / status',

48
00:03:48,763 --> 00:03:56,310
without caring who reads it; dashboards, MES and databases each subscribe,

49
00:03:56,330 --> 00:03:58,559
and a broker forwards.

50
00:03:58,959 --> 00:04:01,534
Neither side knows the other,

51
00:04:01,554 --> 00:04:07,619
so adding a dashboard or a database changes nothing on the machines.

52
00:04:08,019 --> 00:04:13,055
The headers are tiny, so Wi-Fi and small modules cope.

53
00:04:14,575 --> 00:04:17,915
MQTT has three levels of service.

54
00:04:18,315 --> 00:04:22,391
QoS 0 delivers at most once and may lose;

55
00:04:22,411 --> 00:04:30,284
QoS 1 at least once and may duplicate; QoS 2 exactly once, at the highest cost.

56
00:04:30,684 --> 00:04:34,327
Counters and events normally use QoS 1,

57
00:04:34,347 --> 00:04:40,525
so the receiver de-duplicates by the unique ID inside the message;

58
00:04:40,545 --> 00:04:45,784
the packet identifier gets reused and can't do that job.

59
00:04:46,184 --> 00:04:51,564
Cumulative counters help again: a duplicate never double-counts.

60
00:04:53,084 --> 00:04:57,664
A few MQTT features matter on the floor.

61
00:04:58,064 --> 00:05:03,022
Retained messages: the broker keeps the latest per topic,

62
00:05:03,042 --> 00:05:08,524
so a newly opened dashboard gets every machine's state at once.

63
00:05:08,924 --> 00:05:11,776
Will messages: left at connect time,

64
00:05:11,796 --> 00:05:15,685
sent by the broker when the keep-alive times out,

65
00:05:15,705 --> 00:05:21,190
so the dashboard shows 'offline'; a clean disconnect doesn't fire it,

66
00:05:21,210 --> 00:05:23,424
so publish offline yourself.

67
00:05:23,824 --> 00:05:29,124
Wildcards: plus matches one level, hash everything below.

68
00:05:30,644 --> 00:05:35,786
How topics are named decides how easy data are to find.

69
00:05:36,186 --> 00:05:40,162
Follow ISA-95, plant, area, station, category,

70
00:05:40,182 --> 00:05:45,721
and have every system use one topic tree: the unified namespace,

71
00:05:45,741 --> 00:05:50,846
which makes the broker the plant's single real-time source.

72
00:05:51,246 --> 00:06:00,553
Every WenQuest message is the same JSON: an envelope with version, ID, time,

73
00:06:00,573 --> 00:06:06,689
type and source, and the business content in data.

74
00:06:07,089 --> 00:06:10,309
Simulated and real machines differ only in source.

75
00:06:11,829 --> 00:06:14,423
Data volume and metrics.

76
00:06:15,943 --> 00:06:23,730
One lockstitch machine turns its spindle about 120 minutes a shift,

77
00:06:23,750 --> 00:06:27,226
roughly 360 thousand stitches.

78
00:06:27,626 --> 00:06:32,339
You can report every stitch, or aggregate first.

79
00:06:32,739 --> 00:06:39,012
Now 500 machines, two shifts, 300 days, 250 bytes a message;

80
00:06:39,032 --> 00:06:45,305
the chart cycles through the five strategies on a log scale.

81
00:06:45,705 --> 00:06:51,467
Every stitch: about 27 terabytes a year, mostly unread.

82
00:06:51,867 --> 00:06:56,605
Every ten minutes: stoppages show ten minutes late.

83
00:06:57,005 --> 00:07:04,046
The sensible way aggregates counters at the edge and reports state changes at once:

84
00:07:04,066 --> 00:07:10,086
about 36 to 45 gigabytes a year, with stoppages shown within 5 seconds.

85
00:07:11,606 --> 00:07:18,506
OEE measures a station overall by splitting its losses three ways.

86
00:07:18,906 --> 00:07:22,480
A deducts stoppages: waiting,

87
00:07:22,500 --> 00:07:30,534
faults and about 11 thread breaks leave about 402 of 450 minutes,

88
00:07:30,554 --> 00:07:33,136
A about 89.3 percent.

89
00:07:33,536 --> 00:07:43,316
P deducts speed loss: 670 pieces due, 569 made, P 85 percent.

90
00:07:43,716 --> 00:07:48,216
Q deducts rework: 97 percent.

91
00:07:48,616 --> 00:07:53,436
Multiplied: about 73.6 percent.

92
00:07:54,956 --> 00:08:02,389
This is the OEE waterfall: from 450 planned minutes, step down through waiting,

93
00:08:02,409 --> 00:08:06,257
faults and thread breaks to running time,

94
00:08:06,277 --> 00:08:12,389
then through speed loss and rework to the green good-output time,

95
00:08:12,409 --> 00:08:14,087
about 331 minutes.

96
00:08:14,487 --> 00:08:18,227
The biggest loss is speed, about 60 minutes.

97
00:08:18,627 --> 00:08:23,893
One caution: P uses a standard minute that includes handling,

98
00:08:23,913 --> 00:08:26,926
so it measures operator efficiency,

99
00:08:26,946 --> 00:08:33,078
and a manual station's OEE can't be compared directly with a machine's.

100
00:08:34,598 --> 00:08:39,012
A case written from first principles.

101
00:08:39,412 --> 00:08:45,234
A shop counted pieces as trims divided by seams and paid piece rates on it,

102
00:08:45,254 --> 00:08:49,128
and operators found the count above their tickets.

103
00:08:49,528 --> 00:08:56,451
Re-sewing and restarting after a break each add a trim:

104
00:08:56,471 --> 00:09:03,899
about 1747 trims over 3 gives 582 pieces, 2.3 percent high.

105
00:09:04,299 --> 00:09:09,519
Missed ticket scans lose whole bundles; hangers are most accurate.

106
00:09:09,919 --> 00:09:14,099
Count by hanger or ticket and check with trims.

107
00:09:15,619 --> 00:09:18,679
Integration, twins and security.

108
00:09:20,199 --> 00:09:26,657
A digital twin is a virtual floor that changes in step with the real one,

109
00:09:26,677 --> 00:09:27,899
in two layers.

110
00:09:28,299 --> 00:09:31,318
On the left, the real-time mirror:

111
00:09:31,338 --> 00:09:37,039
every station and hanger has a counterpart updated from the bus.

112
00:09:37,439 --> 00:09:41,907
On the right, prediction: start from the actual state,

113
00:09:41,927 --> 00:09:47,225
fast-forward the next few hours to see which operation will jam,

114
00:09:47,245 --> 00:09:49,220
and compare a few fixes.

115
00:09:49,620 --> 00:09:53,754
Once connected, these models run on measured data.

116
00:09:55,274 --> 00:09:58,054
All of this carries over to robots.

117
00:09:58,454 --> 00:10:05,271
A welding or handling robot publishes its state as a retained message on its

118
00:10:05,291 --> 00:10:06,441
status topic,

119
00:10:06,461 --> 00:10:09,590
turned 'offline' by a will message,

120
00:10:09,610 --> 00:10:13,818
and reports cycle and pick counts cumulatively.

121
00:10:14,218 --> 00:10:19,366
Its OEE is the same A times P times Q,

122
00:10:19,386 --> 00:10:25,078
with P based on the program's ideal cycle.

123
00:10:25,478 --> 00:10:28,082
Counting has the same trap:

124
00:10:28,102 --> 00:10:33,524
count by gripper closures and every re-grip adds a part,

125
00:10:33,544 --> 00:10:38,577
so cross-check with vision or a part-present signal.

126
00:10:40,097 --> 00:10:45,170
There's an exact everyday parallel: the smart electricity meter.

127
00:10:45,570 --> 00:10:50,710
It reports the running total on its register, not how much was used just now.

128
00:10:51,110 --> 00:10:53,943
If one report is lost, no matter:

129
00:10:53,963 --> 00:11:01,033
the next reading minus the last one received still gives the usage for the period,

130
00:11:01,053 --> 00:11:04,492
and a duplicate doesn't add to the bill.

131
00:11:04,892 --> 00:11:11,235
A sewing machine reporting cumulative stitches and pieces relies on the very

132
00:11:11,255 --> 00:11:12,072
same idea.

133
00:11:13,592 --> 00:11:15,519
Back to our problem.

134
00:11:15,919 --> 00:11:23,385
Running time is about 402 minutes, A about 89.3 percent;

135
00:11:23,405 --> 00:11:31,807
about 569 pieces were made, P 85, Q 97, OEE about 73.6 percent.

136
00:11:32,207 --> 00:11:35,947
The biggest loss is speed, about 60 minutes.

137
00:11:36,347 --> 00:11:44,369
Counting trims gives 582, 2.3 percent high.

138
00:11:44,769 --> 00:11:51,585
Per-stitch reporting from 500 machines is about 27 terabytes a year;

139
00:11:51,605 --> 00:11:56,410
aggregated with events, only 36 to 45 gigabytes.

140
00:11:57,930 --> 00:12:01,484
Now Virtual lab 26-1, a connected sewing machine,

141
00:12:01,504 --> 00:12:05,203
which you can do yourself on WenQuest. The results,

142
00:12:05,223 --> 00:12:07,464
criteria and tasks are blurred.

143
00:12:07,484 --> 00:12:12,278
Page one shows the event stream and the OEE waterfall on the left,

144
00:12:12,298 --> 00:12:15,924
with a bus message in the WenQuest envelope below;

145
00:12:15,944 --> 00:12:18,550
you set the conditions on the right.

146
00:12:18,950 --> 00:12:23,933
First reset to standard and read the pieces, A, P, Q and OEE.

147
00:12:23,953 --> 00:12:26,967
Then click 'New stretch' a few times,

148
00:12:26,987 --> 00:12:30,412
count the pieces and trims in one stretch,

149
00:12:30,432 --> 00:12:33,774
and explain why the trim count runs high.

150
00:12:34,174 --> 00:12:39,362
Task two: leave the standard minute, efficiency and thread breaks alone,

151
00:12:39,382 --> 00:12:45,004
change only how pieces are counted, together with the missed-scan probability,

152
00:12:45,024 --> 00:12:49,055
and get the count error under 1 percent. How you set it,

153
00:12:49,075 --> 00:12:52,310
and why the error drops, goes in your report.

154
00:12:52,710 --> 00:12:58,589
Task one: on page two, keep 500 machines, 250 bytes a message and two shifts,

155
00:12:58,609 --> 00:13:03,876
try the five reporting strategies one by one, and check message rate,

156
00:13:03,896 --> 00:13:05,025
yearly storage,

157
00:13:05,045 --> 00:13:09,928
state delay and count delay to find the ones that pass all four.

158
00:13:09,948 --> 00:13:15,827
Task three asks you to work out OEE by hand from the shift data at the bottom

159
00:13:15,847 --> 00:13:16,747
of the page.

160
00:13:16,767 --> 00:13:20,577
The guide and report template are on the platform.

161
00:13:22,097 --> 00:13:24,672
This is the WenQuest digital factory,

162
00:13:24,692 --> 00:13:27,687
where every rule from today is put to work.

163
00:13:27,707 --> 00:13:32,876
The adjustment station on the assembly line measures the hook clearance of

164
00:13:32,896 --> 00:13:33,858
every machine,

165
00:13:33,878 --> 00:13:36,382
and each reading is one bus message.

166
00:13:36,782 --> 00:13:43,330
Look at the message box below. The topic runs plant, area, station, category,

167
00:13:43,350 --> 00:13:50,068
level by level. Outside is the common envelope, with ID, time, type and source;

168
00:13:50,088 --> 00:13:54,844
inside 'data' are the serial number, the characteristic,

169
00:13:54,864 --> 00:13:59,706
the reading and the hook batch. The historian subscribes,

170
00:13:59,726 --> 00:14:05,762
draws the control chart, raises an alarm as soon as the process drifts,

171
00:14:05,782 --> 00:14:08,747
and drafts a nonconformance report.

172
00:14:09,147 --> 00:14:10,847
Page two is traceability:

173
00:14:10,867 --> 00:14:15,111
from the machine that raised the alarm back to its hook batch,

174
00:14:15,131 --> 00:14:20,683
then forward to every machine built with that batch and whether any have shipped.

175
00:14:20,703 --> 00:14:24,604
It works because every message carries the serial number.

176
00:14:25,004 --> 00:14:30,350
Page three is the roll-out plan: how many phases, what each costs,

177
00:14:30,370 --> 00:14:36,366
and how long until it pays back. The next lecture walks through all of it.

178
00:14:37,886 --> 00:14:41,758
Your assignment has four questions. One:

179
00:14:41,778 --> 00:14:49,348
a station works a 480-minute shift with a 30-minute break, 40 minutes waiting,

180
00:14:49,368 --> 00:14:56,744
20 of faults, 2 one-minute thread breaks an hour, a standard minute of 0.75,

181
00:14:56,764 --> 00:15:02,582
80 percent efficiency, 4 percent rework and 4 seams a piece;

182
00:15:02,602 --> 00:15:08,421
the full data are on the platform. Find the pieces, A, P, Q,

183
00:15:08,441 --> 00:15:14,746
OEE and the trim-count error, and say which loss to tackle first.

184
00:15:15,146 --> 00:15:20,294
Two: for a floor of 300 machines, choose a reporting strategy,

185
00:15:20,314 --> 00:15:24,045
estimate the message rate and yearly storage,

186
00:15:24,065 --> 00:15:27,046
and check each of the four criteria.

187
00:15:27,446 --> 00:15:31,382
Three: design the topics for line 2, station 5,

188
00:15:31,402 --> 00:15:37,105
write one counter message in JSON, and explain the retained message,

189
00:15:37,125 --> 00:15:39,798
the will and the wildcard. Four:

190
00:15:39,818 --> 00:15:45,858
the floor wants to publish a ranking of every operator's time per piece;

191
00:15:45,878 --> 00:15:49,477
write an opinion of no more than 300 words.

192
00:15:49,497 --> 00:15:54,695
Remember that operators' personal data need notice of purpose,

193
00:15:54,715 --> 00:16:00,586
means and scope up front, and only what is necessary may be collected.

194
00:16:00,986 --> 00:16:06,296
Submit the assignment and the lab report on the WenQuest platform.

195
00:16:07,816 --> 00:16:12,269
There's also a ten-question quiz. For example:

196
00:16:12,289 --> 00:16:19,174
counter messages go out with QoS 1, so the receiver may get duplicates.

197
00:16:19,194 --> 00:16:22,189
What should it de-duplicate by?

198
00:16:22,589 --> 00:16:28,113
Or: 800 sewing machines each send one counter summary a minute.

199
00:16:28,133 --> 00:16:32,689
How many messages per second does the floor average?

200
00:16:33,089 --> 00:16:38,476
You get three attempts, and the explanations appear as soon as you submit.

201
00:16:39,996 --> 00:16:42,216
Three things to remember.

202
00:16:42,616 --> 00:16:49,231
One: machine data come as identity, state, counters and events;

203
00:16:49,251 --> 00:16:55,128
counters are cumulative and de-duplicated by message ID.

204
00:16:55,528 --> 00:16:55,903
Two:

205
00:16:55,923 --> 00:17:04,111
MQTT with a unified namespace makes the broker the plant's single real-time source;

206
00:17:04,131 --> 00:17:11,428
retained messages give current state, will messages mark machines offline.

207
00:17:11,828 --> 00:17:19,946
Three: aggregate counters at the edge and report state changes at once;

208
00:17:19,966 --> 00:17:27,627
OEE is A times P times Q, and trim counts run high, so cross-check.

209
00:17:28,027 --> 00:17:30,599
The slides, the virtual lab,

210
00:17:30,619 --> 00:17:37,082
the assignment and the quiz are all on the WenQuest learning platform,

211
00:17:37,102 --> 00:17:43,287
at learn.wenquestrobotics.com. Sign up for free and start learning.

212
00:17:43,686 --> 00:17:44,675
See you next time.
